1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,542
Bring back three items. It is a familiar
request in games.

2
00:00:03,542 --> 00:00:07,122
Some errands make you curious about the next
destination,

3
00:00:07,122 --> 00:00:09,552
while others just make you want to finish.

4
00:00:09,552 --> 00:00:14,002
The difference may lie in what happens between
the instruction and delivery.

5
00:00:14,002 --> 00:00:17,972
We will compare travel and gathering in Skyrim
with resource exploration in Subnautica,

6
00:00:17,972 --> 00:00:21,402
then change conditions in an original delivery
prototype.

7
00:00:21,402 --> 00:00:23,392
We will examine three questions.

8
00:00:23,392 --> 00:00:27,222
What choices happen along the way? What
changes after delivery?

9
00:00:27,222 --> 00:00:30,320
And does the return journey offer anything
new?

10
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:34,142
First, look at the actions on the way, rather
than only the destination.

11
00:00:34,142 --> 00:00:37,362
When gathering materials in Subnautica, you
inspect your surroundings

12
00:00:37,362 --> 00:00:41,462
and use the remaining oxygen to decide whether
to keep searching or surface.

13
00:00:41,462 --> 00:00:43,942
This is not a quest assigned by a character,

14
00:00:43,942 --> 00:00:47,892
but an example of a player seeking materials
for their own needs.

15
00:00:47,892 --> 00:00:50,922
A gathering goal can contain observation and
decisions.

16
00:00:50,922 --> 00:00:54,672
If following a marker leaves no action or
route to choose,

17
00:00:54,672 --> 00:00:58,702
extra travel time may add waiting rather than
meaningful activity.

18
00:00:58,702 --> 00:01:02,912
Write down the moments when players make
decisions during your quest.

19
00:01:02,912 --> 00:01:06,720
Check whether those choices actually exist
between destinations.

20
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:10,213
Next, examine what changes when the task is
completed.

21
00:01:10,213 --> 00:01:13,273
Here are travel and mining scenes from Skyrim.

22
00:01:13,273 --> 00:01:17,343
This footage does not demonstrate the reward
of a particular quest.

23
00:01:17,343 --> 00:01:20,183
It illustrates the actions of travel and
gathering.

24
00:01:20,183 --> 00:01:23,283
The question for our own game is separate.

25
00:01:23,283 --> 00:01:26,113
After delivering the items, does only the
score increase?

26
00:01:26,113 --> 00:01:29,233
Or does a route open, or someone behave
differently?

27
00:01:29,233 --> 00:01:33,693
A numerical reward can matter, but a visible
consequence

28
00:01:33,693 --> 00:01:36,863
may help players understand why the task
mattered.

29
00:01:36,863 --> 00:01:40,320
We will build that difference in a small
delivery game.

30
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:44,863
Both versions ask you to collect three logs
along the same route and return.

31
00:01:44,863 --> 00:01:48,033
Movement speed, item positions, and the score
reward are identical.

32
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:51,393
In the first version, delivery raises the
score and completes the request.

33
00:01:51,393 --> 00:01:54,893
In the second, the same delivery repairs a
broken bridge.

34
00:01:54,893 --> 00:01:57,863
The places you can reach afterward actually
change.

35
00:01:57,863 --> 00:02:01,493
The bridge is more than decorative completion
artwork.

36
00:02:01,493 --> 00:02:05,183
A blocked collision area opens, allowing the
character to cross.

37
00:02:05,183 --> 00:02:08,753
This comparison demonstrates a change in the
world state.

38
00:02:08,753 --> 00:02:12,803
Whether the second version is more enjoyable
still needs human playtesting.

39
00:02:12,803 --> 00:02:16,320
A successful automated input trace is not an
enjoyment rating.

40
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:18,873
Now examine the return journey.

41
00:02:18,873 --> 00:02:23,553
You initially take a detour, but repairing the
bridge opens a shorter route.

42
00:02:23,553 --> 00:02:26,593
The same location now has a different role.

43
00:02:26,593 --> 00:02:31,423
Another test adds a gate on the short route
that requires an empty inventory.

44
00:02:31,423 --> 00:02:35,833
You choose between returning quickly and
carrying the item along the longer route.

45
00:02:35,833 --> 00:02:39,343
The presence of a shortcut alone does not
create a decision.

46
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:42,823
Players need to understand what they gain and
give up.

47
00:02:42,823 --> 00:02:46,553
You do not need an event during every moment
of travel.

48
00:02:46,553 --> 00:02:51,223
Quiet time can allow players to notice the
surroundings or reflect.

49
00:02:51,223 --> 00:02:54,560
First decide what purpose a repeated stretch
should serve.

50
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:58,973
When designing a quest, add three lines
beneath its objective.

51
00:02:58,973 --> 00:03:03,613
List the choices on the way, the change after
completion, and what is new on return.

52
00:03:03,613 --> 00:03:06,773
If all three are empty, before adding more
requests,

53
00:03:06,773 --> 00:03:09,163
introduce a small change in one stretch of the
journey.

54
00:03:09,163 --> 00:03:13,603
Ask players where they paused and which route
they chose.

55
00:03:13,603 --> 00:03:16,743
Check what they thought changed after
completion.

56
00:03:16,743 --> 00:03:20,163
Their understanding may differ from your
design intent.

57
00:03:20,163 --> 00:03:23,313
The number of items alone does not determine a
good errand.

58
00:03:23,313 --> 00:03:26,123
Examine the decisions players make inside a
small request

59
00:03:26,123 --> 00:03:28,640
alongside the changes those decisions leave
behind.
